t six months so that she  might successfully bring the flailing  company back to life at one point a  reporter explains umbrella's past and  present as a company besieged by legal  Scandal and falling stock prices is now  trying to reinvent itself with a major  push into direct-to-consumer drugs the  allusion to both Purdue and insist  Therapeutics an out bankrupt company  responsible for fentanyl is both clear  and cunning equally clear in their  commentary or the opioid withdrawal  mirroring side effects of joy and a  zombifying effect that high doses of the  drug elicit  so we know what goes on the warning  label  the line from Evelyn is a biting  reference the big Pharma consistently  blaming the user of a drug while  ignoring their own irresponsible  marketing to that drug is blindly  apparent the fact that umbrella's latest  weapon is manipulation and controlled  through dependency rather than monstrous  Brute Force marks a major turning point  for the narrative but in addition to  this fresh Twist on the story there's an  important thematic tie-in to the series  Source material  Netflix's Resident Evil also highlights  the gaslighting tactics used by some of  today's Tech and Pharma Giants it's for  your own good umbrella insists as do our  real world adds absent pharmaceutical  companies combine this sales tactic with  the initially good intentions that spark  so many advances in Science and Tech and  you create a dynamic wherein both seller  and buyer are locked in a one-way Road  to Hell this idea is most thoroughly  shown in the last two episodes of  Resident Evil when we learned that for  all her flippant disregard of Joy users  and for all her power hungry ambition  Evelyn might genuinely believe that her  creation could change the world as she  says several times for the better  there's more than a few shades of the  tragic tale of theranos and Evelyn's  repeated use of that phrase but in  having the umbrella CEO allow her  ambition to overtake a regard for human  life and autonomy Resident Evil is once  again presenting us with a cautionary  Tale in the penultimate episode we  learned that Evelyn has been drugging  her wife with joy in order to keep her  compliant this marks the point of no  return for the wife and mother who will  later continue her Mission despite the  loss of her son  in contrast to Evelyn The Wesker of  Netflix's Resident Evil never loses his  Humanity differing wildly from the  one-dimensional villain of the games and  movies the hard-working dad with whom  were presented for the majority of the  series is not we learn in the end the  actual Albert Wesker when the original  Wesker needed a team of scientists to  help him Advance the virus he created a  handful of Wesker clones only two of  these clones survive bur to Evelyn keeps  locked up and Albert the Dad we believe  is Wesker for most of the show but they  have serious health problems as a result  of Aging instantaneously in order to  stay alive Albert creates his two  daughters Billy and Jade and uses their  blood to Stave off his symptoms it's a  simultaneously nasty and nuanced kind of  Parental need Albert does love his  daughters as evidenced by his  willingness to do anything to protect  them here again the series is  highlighting The Not So straightforward  nature of our push-pull relationship  with scientific progress yes Wesker is  using the girls but he has no choice in  the matter if he wants to live he does  however have a choice as to whether or  not he'll sacrifice himself for her this  Gap and what he can and can't control  his human in weakness versus his human  strengths Mira's society's complicated  needed for pharmaceutical breakthroughs  in the end we learned that adult Billy  has joined umbrella eventually finding a  way to control even Evelyn Jade has gone  in a different direction working for a  rebel University and studying the  behavior of the zombies that now  Encompass the planet sister is pitted  against sister as it's revealed that  Billy like her clone dad before her  needs Jade's blood in order to survive  and one of the most important final  scenes of the series we witnessed Billy  turning to Evelyn for help  if I am sick  can you help me it's not hard to see  this cause of the sickness owner of the  Cure Dynamic and the call for companies  like Facebook to fix the monster they  created unfortunately as data scientist  Kathy O'Neill points out in the social  dilemma this is a misguided ask  we are allowing the technologists to  frame this as a problem that they are  equipped to solve  that is that's a lie Evelyn and umbrella  may be able to keep Billy alive but they  do so at the cost of her very Humanity  in the end Billy's decisions rely on the  corporation leads to her willingness to  shoot her sister in the stomach and  kidnap her niece to mind for blood as  Albert struggles to reject Evelyn's  control and the build-up to the outbreak  she reminds him that willpower has  nothing to do with his current situation  you need your medicine she says about an  hour maybe less  pain will become unbearable and you'll  give in  later as he lies dying on the floor  begging his daughters for forgiveness  Evelyn leans over him and places a  syringe of Jade's blood in his hand it's  a heartbreaking scene and one that  directly reflects the Dynamics between  an addict their family and their  supplier despite the gutting symbolism  of Albert's final moments before he  sacrifices himself to free his daughters  the series eventually leaves us with  some semblance of Hope  when adult Jade unleashes a  mountain-sized mutated crocodile on  umbrella's base camp it's not simply a  reptilian display of gamer fan service  the confrontation of past and future of  Nature and technology and of history and  progress are all brought to the  foreground in the series's final moments  the fact that umbrella's technology is  able to tear at the body of a creature  that predates our existence is